fieldsmith 1
# === FIELDSMITH LITERATE CASE ===
# @id: A3
# @code: A3
# @category: analytic
# @level: Analytic Verification
# @title: Analytic Benchmark A3: De Laval Converging–Diverging Nozzle Shell Volume vs Pappus & Conical Frusta (V4 / V60a)
# @subtitle: Verify Fieldsmith’s axisymmetric revolve SDF primitive and sub-voxel occupancy volume integrator against the exact closed-form conical frusta sum and Pappus’s second centroid theorem.
# @verification_badge: Verified by V4 & V60(a) · Exact Pappus / Frusta Volume · PASS (0.27%)
# @doc_path: docs/analytic-tests/A3-de-laval-revolve-pappus-volume.md
# @fsm_path: examples/analytic/A3-de-laval-revolve-pappus-volume.fsm
# @starter_fsm_name: a3-de-laval-revolve-volume.fsm
# @verification_refs: docs/verification/V4.md, docs/verification/V60.md
# ---
# @check: Cylindrical Chamber Section Volume (z ∈ [0, 80] mm, r ∈ [18, 26] mm) || V₁ = π · 80 · (26² − 18²) = 28,160 π mm³ || 88,467.25 mm³ || Exact Algebraic Anchor || 0.000% || Pappus Identity
# @check: Converging + Diverging Conical Frusta Volume (z ∈ [80, 140] mm) || V₂ + V₃ = 2π r̄₂ A₂ + 2π r̄₃ A₃ = 15,360 π mm³ || 48,254.86 mm³ || Exact Algebraic Anchor || 0.000% || Pappus Identity
# @check: Total De Laval Shell SDF Integrated Volume (V₁ + V₂ + V₃) || V_exact = 43,520 π mm³;  m_CuCrZr = ρ · V = 8.90 g/cm³ × 136.722 cm³ = 1,216.83 g || 136,722.11 mm³ (1,216.8 g) || 136,358.73 mm³ (1,213.6 g) || 0.266% || ≤ 0.50%
# ---
# @assert: Pappus De Laval Shell Mass m_CuCrZr || mass_g || 1216.83 || 1.0% || g
# @assert: Test Pressure Peak Stress Bound || max_vm_elem || <= 15.0 || <= 15.0 || MPa
# ---
# @step: 1. Inspect the 3-Stage De Laval Revolve Profile in Shape (1) || shape
#   Open Shape (1). The shell is defined by subtracting two 2D meridional polygon profiles revolved 360° around the Z axis: (1) a cylindrical combustion chamber z ∈ [0, 80] mm with r_i = 18 mm, r_o = 26 mm; (2) a converging conical section z ∈ [80, 110] mm tapering to r_i = 7.5 mm, r_o = 15.5 mm at the sonic throat; and (3) a diverging bell cone z ∈ [110, 140] mm expanding to r_i = 15 mm, r_o = 23 mm.
# @expected:
#   Both revolve primitives have a constant radial wall thickness t_r = R_o(z) − R_i(z) = 8.0 mm across all three axial stations.
# @step: 2. Verify Volume & Mass Readout Against Pappus’s Second Centroid Theorem || results
#   By Pappus’s second theorem, the volume swept by revolving a meridional lamina of area A_k = t_r Δz_k about the Z axis is V_k = 2π r̄_k A_k: V₁ = 2π(22)(640) = 28,160π mm³, V₂ = 2π(16.75)(240) = 8,040π mm³, and V₃ = 2π(15.25)(240) = 7,320π mm³, giving V_exact = 43,520π = 136,722.11 mm³ (mass = 1,216.8 g at ρ = 8.90 g/cm³ for CuCrZr). Switch to Results (3) to compare the integrated mass/volume readout.
# @expected:
#   Fieldsmith’s sub-voxel SDF integrator matches the exact 43,520π mm³ analytical volume within 0.27%.
# ---
# @theory_title: Analytic Verification A3 — Axisymmetric Revolve SDF & Sub-Voxel Occupancy Integration (V4 / V60a)
# @theory_summary:
#   Shows how Fieldsmith evaluates exact Euclidean signed distance d(x, y, z) to a 2D polygon in the (r = √(x²+y²), z) meridional half-plane and integrates volume with linear sub-voxel gradient occupancy.
# @theory_section: A3.1 Meridional Reduction & Pappus–Conical Frustum Equivalence
# @theory_eq:
#   r = √(x² + y²),     d_revolve(x, y, z) = d_polygon2D(r, z)
#   V_frustum = (π h / 3)[(R_o1² + R_o1 R_o2 + R_o2²) − (R_i1² + R_i1 R_i2 + R_i2²)] = 2π r̄ A_meridional
# @theory_body:
#   Because Euclidean distance is invariant under orthogonal rotation around Z, the 3D signed distance to any surface of revolution equals the 2D signed distance to its generating polygon in the (r, z) plane.
# ---
# @mcp: Inspect De Laval Shell Geometry & Mass via API || inspect || {}
#   Query the document’s shapes and solved mass/volume telemetry.
# === END LITERATE HEADER ===

name "A3 — De Laval Revolve Shell Volume Benchmark (V60a)"
material cucrzr

revolve outer_wall profile=[(0, 0), (26, 0), (26, 80), (15.5, 110), (23, 140), (0, 140)]
revolve gas_bore profile=[(0, -1), (18, -1), (18, 80), (7.5, 110), (15, 140), (15, 141), (0, 141)] subtract
region flange = outer_wall.bottom
region chamber = gas_bore.side
support hold flange fixed=xyz
load test_p chamber pressure=2.0
solve
